¶
'''wax-scot†''' (waks′skot), ''n.'' A tax or money payment made by parishioners to supply the church with wax candles.

'''wax-tree''' (waks′trē), ''n.'' One of several trees, of different localities, the source of some kind of vegetable or insect wax. {{smaller|(''a'') The Japan wax-tree, specifically ''Rhus succedanea'', a small tree originally from the Loochoo Islands, now extensively planted in Japan, especially on the borders of fields, for its small clustered berries, which yield by expression an excellent candle-wax. The lacquer-tree, ''Rhus vernicifera'', yields a still better wax. (''b'') In China, one of several trees yielding the pela, or white wax (see ''wax''<sup>2</sup>), which incrusts their twigs as the result of the puncture of an insect. One of the most important is a species of privet, ''Ligustrum lucidum''; another is an ash, ''Fraxinus Chinensis''. ''Ligustrum Ibota'' appears to furnish a variety of the same product. (''c'') A plant of the genus ''Vismia'', which consists of trees and shrubs abounding in a yellow resinous juice. This is collected from some South American species, particularly ''V. Guianensis'', and from its qualities is sometimes called ''American gamboge''. (''d'') The Colombian varnish-tree, ''Elæagia utilis''. (''e'') The wax-myrtle, ''Myrica cerifera''. [Rare.]}}
{{smaller block|A fragrant shrub, called the Anemiche by the Indians, had attracted the attention of the government. It is the ''wax-tree'', or candle-berry (Myrica cerifera), of which the wax is used for making candles.
{{right|''Gayarré'', Hist. Louisiana, I. 520.}}}}

'''wax-weed''' (waks′wēd), ''n.'' An American herb, ''Cuphea viscosissima'', sometimes designated as ''clammy cuphea''. {{smaller|It is a branching plant with purple stems covered with extremely viscid hairs; the petals of the small flowers are also purple. The full name is ''blue wax-weed''.}}

'''waxwing''' (waks′wing), ''n.'' An oscine passerine bird of the genus ''Ampelis'' (or ''Bombycilla''), fam-

readings.c2s2:
ily ''Ampelidæ:'' so called because the secondary quills of the wings, and sometimes other feathers of the wings or tail, are tipped with small red horny appendages resembling sealing-wax. {{smaller|There are three species—the Bohemian waxwing or chatterer, ''A. garrulus,'' of the northern hemisphere generally,
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Bohemian Waxwing (''Ampelis garrulus'').}}}}
breeding in high latitudes, and migrating southward irregularly, sometimes in flocks of vast extent; the red-winged Japanese waxwing, ''A. phœnicoptera;'' and the smaller Carolina waxwing, cedar-bird, cedar-lark, cherry-bird, etc., of North America, ''A. cedrorum,'' the prib chatterer of Latham, 1785. The sealing-wax tips are the enlarged, hardened, and peculiarly modified prolongation of the shaft of the feather, composed of central and peripheral substances differing in the shape of the pigment-cells, which contain abundance of red and yellow coloring matter. Their use is unknown.}}

'''waxwork''' (waks′wėrk), ''n.'' '''1.''' Work in wax;

readings.c3s1:
Specifically—'''2.''' Noting certain complexions. {{smaller|(''a'') Pallid or blanched; of a translucent pallor, as in bloodlessness. (''b'') Of a dull, pasty, whitish color, sometimes inclining to the yellowishness of raw beeswax. This is a complexion almost diagnostic of the so-called scrofulous or cancerous diathesis, and of persons in whom the opium habit is confirmed and of long standing.}}

'''3.''' Made of wax; abounding in wax; waxed: as, a ''waxy'' dressing for leather.—'''Waxy degeneration.''' {{smaller|(''a'') Same as ''lardaceous disease'' (which see, under ''lardaceous''). (''b'') A change of parts of the muscular fibers into a peculiar hyaline substance, which differs from lardacein; it occurs in certain cases of typhoid fever, meningitis, and other acute febrile disorders.}}—'''Waxy liver, kidney, spleen,''' {{smaller|etc., a liver, kidney, spleen, etc., which has undergone waxy degeneration.}}

'''waxy<sup>2</sup>''' (wak′si), ''a.'' [< ''wax''<sup>3</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Angry; wrathy; irate. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|It would cheer him up more than anything if I could make him a little ''waxy'' with me.
{{right|''Dickens'', Bleak House, xxiv.}}}}

'''way<sup>1</sup>''' (wā), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''waye'', ''waie''; < ME. ''way'', ''wai'', ''wey'', ''wei'', ''weye'', ''weie'', ''wæi'', < AS. ''weg'' = OS. ''weg'' = OFries. ''wei'' = MD. ''wegh'', D. ''weg'' = MLG. LG. ''weg'' = OHG. MHG. ''wec'', G. ''weg'' = Icel. ''vegr'' = Sw. ''väg'' = Dan. ''vej'' = Goth. ''wigs'', a way, road, = L. ''via'', OL. ''vea'', orig. *''veha'' = Lith. ''weza'', track of a cart, = Skt. ''vaha'', a road, way; from the verb represented by AS. ''wegan'', etc., bear, carry, = L. ''vehere'', carry, = Skt. √ ''vah'', carry: see ''weigh''<sup>1</sup>. From the same verb are ult. E. ''wain''<sup>1</sup> and ''wagon'', etc., and, from the L., ''vehicle'', etc. For the E. words from L. ''via'', see

readings.c3s2:
''via''<sup>1</sup>. Hence ''away'' (reduced to ''way''<sup>2</sup>), and ''wayward'', etc.] '''1.''' The track or path by passing over or along which some place has been or may be reached; a course leading from one place to another; a road; a street; a passage, channel, or route; a line of march, progression, or motion: as, the ''way'' to market or to school; a broad or a narrow ''way''.
{{smaller block|Men seyn that the Wlcanes ben ''Weyes'' of Helle.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 55.}}
<poem>A grene ''wey'' thou schalt fynde,
That geth as euene as he may to paradys the on ende;
Ther biȝonde thi Modur and ich.</poem>
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 23.}}
The worst ''wayes'' that ever I travelled in all my life in the Sommer were those betwixt Chamberie and Aiguebelle. {{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 83.}}
I fear I shall never find the ''way'' to church, because the bells hang so far.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, ii. 1.}}
<poem>The road to resolution lies by doubt;
The next ''way'' home ’s the farthest ''way'' about.</poem>
{{right|''Quarles'', Emblems, iv., Epig. 2.}}
I hope our ''way'' does not lie over any of these [hills], for I dread a precipice. {{right|''Cotton'', in Walton’s Angler, ii. 228.}}
<poem>If prince or peer cross Darrell’s ''way'',
He’ll beard him in his pride.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Rokeby, v. 27.}}}}
'''2.''' A passage along some particular path or course; progress; journey; transit; coming or going.
{{smaller block|The Lord . . . will send his angel with thee, and prosper thy ''way''. {{right|Gen. xxiv. 40.}}
<poem>Shut the doors against his ''way''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., iv. 3. 92.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The next day we again set sail, and made the best of our ''way'', till we were forced, by contrary winds, into St. Remo, a very pretty town in the Genoese dominions.
{{right|''Addison'', Remarks on Italy (ed. Bohn), I. 359.}}}}
{{smaller block|The ship (barring accidents) will touch at no other port on her ''way'' out.
{{right|''W. Collins'', Moonstone, vi. 5.}}}}
'''3.''' Length of space; distance: as, the church is but a little ''way'' from here. In this sense, in colloquial use, often erroneously ''ways''.
{{smaller block|Thy servant will go a little ''way'' over Jordan.
{{right|2 Sam. xix. 36.}}}}
{{smaller block|I here first saw the hills a considerable ''way'' off to the east, no hills appearing that way from the parts about Damascus.
{{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, II. i. 138.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I charge thee ride before,
Ever a good ''way'' on before.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}
'''4.''' Direction as of motion or position: as, he comes this ''way''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Now sways it this ''way'', like a mighty sea, . . .
Now sways it that ''way'', like the selfsame sea.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., ii. 5. 5.}}}}
{{smaller block|The Kingdome of Congo is about 600. miles diameter any ''way''.
{{right|''Capt. John Smith'', True Travels, I. 49.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Three Goddesses for this contend;
See, now they descend,
And this ''Way'' they bend.</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', Judgment of Paris.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>O friend! I hear some step of hostile feet,
Moving this ''way'', or hast’ning to the fleet.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, x. 406.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>No two windows look one ''way''
O’er the small sea-water thread
Below them.</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', In a Gondola.}}}}
'''5.''' Path or course in life.
{{smaller block|The ''way'' of transgressors is hard.
{{right|Prov. xiii. 15.}}}}
